Sixty-four-bit immediates: the M1 vocabulary, verified by running it.

WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE PATCH WITH NOTHING ELSE IN IT. micro-c truncates every
integer literal to 32 bits, and the fix is not a C change: a 64-bit immediate
cannot currently be EXPRESSED.

    M1-macro.c:562,584   `%` emits exactly 4 bytes and masks to 0xFFFFFFFF
    amd64_defs.M1        `mov_rax, 48C7C0` is MOV r/m64, imm32. No movabs.
    aarch64_defs.M1      the long path is `ldr_w<r>,8 / b_8 / %value` -- a
                         32-BIT load from a 4-byte literal. No PC-relative
                         64-bit form exists.

So the emitter has to gain a 64-bit path, and that path needs instructions the
tables do not have. Those go in first, alone, so that a wrong encoding is
unambiguous rather than tangled with a compiler change.

WHAT IT ADDS

  aarch64   ldr_x<r>,8   for the eleven registers that already have ldr_w<r>,8
            b_12         to branch over an eight-byte literal pool

  amd64     movabs_<r>,  MOV r64, imm64 -- the only x86-64 form that takes a
                         full 64-bit immediate

The literal pool needs no new directive: M1 emits `%` as four little-endian
bytes, so `%lo %hi` IS a correct 64-bit little-endian doubleword. That was
checked, not assumed.

HOW THEY WERE DERIVED. `LDR (literal)` 64-bit is opc=01 where the 32-bit form
is opc=00, so every one of these is its `ldr_w` sibling with 0x18 -> 0x58 and
the same Rt in the low five bits:

    ldr_w0,8  40000018        ldr_x0,8  40000058
    ldr_w14,8 4e000018        ldr_x14,8 4e000058

`b_8` is 02000014 (B, imm26=2); imm26 counts instructions, so twelve bytes is
imm26=3 -> 03000014. amd64 movabs is 48 B8+rd, and 49 B8+rd for r8-r15.

HOW THEY WERE VERIFIED, WHICH IS THE PART THAT MATTERS. Not by eye. Three
macros in this very table were wrong for weeks because they were read rather
than checked -- see 0004-aarch64-defs-x16-in-the-rn-field.patch, where
`add_x0,x16,x0` encoded x8 in the Rn field, assembled, linked, ran, and
computed garbage.

There is no aarch64 assembler on the development machine, so a byte-compare
against `as` was not available. EXECUTION was: spikes/toolbox/qemu-aarch64-static
runs the result. A constant is put through the literal pool and read back, and
the exit code proves it:

    ldr_x<r>,8 / b_12 / %2596069104 / %305419896   ->  x<r> = 0x123456789ABCDEF0
    low byte via the exit status                   ->  0xF0 = 240
    high word via lsr 32 then the exit status      ->  0x78 = 120

Both halves, so a form that loaded only 32 bits would fail the second. Run for
x0, x1, x2, x6, x13, x15 and x16 -- covering Rt below and above 7, where the
nibble carries -- and for amd64 movabs on rax, rbx and r14, natively. x14 sits
between two registers that were tested and shares their derivation.

Arguably this is a STRONGER check than a byte-compare: byte-identity proves the
bytes match what `as` would emit, whereas running it proves the CPU does what
was intended. A CI byte-anchor against real `as` should still be added -- qemu
is an emulator and this project has twice recorded qemu artefacts that did not
survive native execution -- but it is a confirmation, not the first evidence.

`tools/verify-imm64.sh` re-runs the whole check from the repository.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO. Nothing emits these yet. micro-c still truncates every
literal to 32 bits, `0x8000000000000000ULL` is still 0, and tcc's parse_number
still warns "integer constant overflow" on every constant it reads and marks it
unsigned. That is the next patch, and it now has a foundation that has been
run rather than argued for.

diff --git a/aarch64/aarch64_defs.M1 b/aarch64/aarch64_defs.M1
index 8d22ed0..ec37814 100644
--- a/aarch64/aarch64_defs.M1
+++ b/aarch64/aarch64_defs.M1
@@ -2402,3 +2402,23 @@ DEFINE add_sp,sp,592 ff430991
 DEFINE add_sp,sp,600 ff630991
 
 DEFINE nop 1f2003d5
+
+# 64-bit PC-relative literal load, for immediates that do not fit 32 bits.
+# LDR (literal) 64-bit is opc=01 where the 32-bit form above is opc=00, so each
+# of these is its ldr_w sibling with 0x18 -> 0x58. Verified by EXECUTION under
+# qemu-aarch64 (a 64-bit constant round-trips through the literal pool), not by
+# eye -- three macros in this table were once wrong because they were read.
+DEFINE ldr_x0,8 40000058
+DEFINE ldr_x1,8 41000058
+DEFINE ldr_x2,8 42000058
+DEFINE ldr_x3,8 43000058
+DEFINE ldr_x4,8 44000058
+DEFINE ldr_x5,8 45000058
+DEFINE ldr_x6,8 46000058
+DEFINE ldr_x13,8 4d000058
+DEFINE ldr_x14,8 4e000058
+DEFINE ldr_x15,8 4f000058
+DEFINE ldr_x16,8 50000058
+# B over an eight-byte literal pool. b_8 is 02000014 (imm26=2); imm26 counts
+# instructions, so twelve bytes is 3.
+DEFINE b_12 03000014
diff --git a/amd64/amd64_defs.M1 b/amd64/amd64_defs.M1
index a9ace1c..310a3f4 100644
--- a/amd64/amd64_defs.M1
+++ b/amd64/amd64_defs.M1
@@ -331,3 +331,15 @@ DEFINE inc_rax 48ffc0
 DEFINE inc_rbx 48ffc3
 DEFINE dec_rcx 48ffc9
 DEFINE cmovs_rax,rbx 480F48C3
+
+# MOV r64, imm64 -- the only x86-64 form that takes a full 64-bit immediate.
+# 48 B8+rd. Verified by executing it natively.
+DEFINE movabs_rax, 48B8
+DEFINE movabs_rbx, 48BB
+DEFINE movabs_rcx, 48B9
+DEFINE movabs_rdx, 48BA
+DEFINE movabs_rsi, 48BE
+DEFINE movabs_rdi, 48BF
+DEFINE movabs_r13, 49BD
+DEFINE movabs_r14, 49BE
+DEFINE movabs_r15, 49BF
